{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"abstract-classes","title":"Abstract Classes & Interfaces","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":39,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <em>pure virtual</em> function (<code>= 0</code>) has no implementation in the base class. A class with at least one is <strong>abstract</strong>: it can't be instantiated, only derived from — that's how C++ spells “interface”.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Interface + implementations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nclass Notifier {                                   // interface: only pure virtuals\npublic:\n    virtual ~Notifier() = default;\n    virtual void send(const std::string&amp; msg) = 0;\n    virtual std::string channel() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass EmailNotifier : public Notifier {\npublic:\n    void send(const std::string&amp; msg) override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"email: \" &lt;&lt; msg &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    std::string channel() const override { return \"email\"; }\n};\n\nclass SmsNotifier : public Notifier {\npublic:\n    void send(const std::string&amp; msg) override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"sms: \" &lt;&lt; msg.substr(0, 10) &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n    std::string channel() const override { return \"sms\"; }\n};\n\nvoid alertAll(const std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Notifier&gt;&gt;&amp; targets, const std::string&amp; msg) {\n    for (const auto&amp; t : targets) t-&gt;send(msg);   // depends only on the interface\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Notifier&gt;&gt; targets;\n    targets.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;EmailNotifier&gt;());\n    targets.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;SmsNotifier&gt;());\n    alertAll(targets, \"Server CPU at 97%\");\n    // Notifier n;   // error: abstract class\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>email: Server CPU at 97%\nsms: Server CPU</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; };\nstruct Square : Shape { double s = 1; };      // forgot to override area → still abstract\nint main() {\n    Square sq;                                // error: variable type 'Square' is an abstract class\n}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Template method pattern</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nclass Report {\npublic:\n    void generate() const {              // fixed algorithm (non-virtual)…\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"== \" &lt;&lt; title() &lt;&lt; \" ==\\n\";\n        body();\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"-- end --\\n\";\n    }\n    virtual ~Report() = default;\nprivate:\n    virtual const char* title() const = 0;    // …customisation points (private virtual is fine)\n    virtual void body() const = 0;\n};\n\nclass SalesReport : public Report {\n    const char* title() const override { return \"Sales\"; }\n    void body() const override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"total: 42\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() { SalesReport{}.generate(); }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>== Sales ==\ntotal: 42\n-- end --</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-info-circle\"></i>Pure virtual with a body</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Base {\n    virtual ~Base() = 0;                         // pure virtual destructor: makes Base abstract…\n    virtual void log() const = 0;\n};\nBase::~Base() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"~Base\\n\"; }       // …but it MUST still have a body\nvoid Base::log() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"default logging\\n\"; }   // optional default implementation\n\nstruct Impl : Base { void log() const override { Base::log(); } };\nint main() { Impl i; i.log(); }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>default logging\n~Base</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Abstract class</th><th>Interface (C++ convention)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pure virtual functions</td><td>At least one</td><td>All (plus virtual dtor)</td></tr><tr><td>Data members</td><td>Allowed</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Implemented functions</td><td>Allowed</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple inheritance</td><td>Possible but careful</td><td>Safe and common</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can an abstract class have a constructor?</summary><div><p>Yes. You can't create a standalone object of it, but its constructor runs as part of constructing every derived object — useful to initialise the base's data members. It's often made <code>protected</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Abstract class vs interface in C++?</summary><div><p>C++ has no <code>interface</code> keyword. An “interface” is just an abstract class with only pure virtual functions, a virtual destructor and no data. An abstract class may additionally carry state and shared implementation.</p></div></details></section>"}